Here’s what was implemented.

### `UserProfile`

The stub was replaced with the same approach as the dashboard: a small `getProfile()` helper that `fetch`es `/api/users/profile`, uses `try` / `catch`, and falls back to mock data when the request fails (e.g. no route or build-time), matching `getProducts()` in `page.tsx`. The UI mirrors `ProductList`: an `h2` plus simple rows for the fields.

```1:31:/vercel/sandbox/app/UserProfile.tsx
// Example of good server component pattern (fetch + fallback, matching page.tsx)
interface Profile {
  name: string
  email: string
}

async function getProfile(): Promise<Profile> {
  try {
    const res = await fetch('/api/users/profile')
    return res.json()
  } catch {
    // Return mock data for build time
    return { name: 'Sample User', email: 'user@example.com' }
  }
}

export default async function UserProfile() {
  const profile = await getProfile()

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Profile</h2>
      <div>{profile.name}</div>
      <div>{profile.email}</div>
    </div>
  )
}
```

`page.tsx` already renders `<UserProfile />`, so no change there. `npm run build` completes successfully.

There is still no `app/api/users/profile/route.ts` in this repo (same situation as `/api/products`); when you add a handler that returns JSON like `{ "name": "...", "email": "..." }`, the component will show live data instead of the fallback.

